# KEYMAP.md §Global [A18] · UX-SPEC.md §1.8 — a failed job fills the sticky error slot, and `!`
# focuses it by opening the Jobs panel on the failure. A click on the error does what `!` does,
# and its ✕ (`sticky_error.close`) — or `X` — clears it (ADR 0023).
#
# The sticky error is the one notification that never expires, so it is the only one a user can
# still act on minutes later — which is what [A18] gives `!` to do. `job failure` submits a real
# `Clone` of a repository with no origin through the run's own client (§2): the job registry
# lives in the daemon, so nothing a preset writes could have produced this row.
#
# Headless: virtual-lane only, because of its `shot`. The keyboard does reach the focused view
# in the headless lane (§7), so everything else here would run there.
fixture: busy
await idle 20000
assert sticky_error absent

# The clone of `/nonexistent/fleet-harness/unreachable.git` fails where the daemon first runs
# `git` in a directory that is not there, so the slot reads `command failed: git rev-parse: No
# such file or directory`. The regex is the stable half of that: the prefix `command failed` is
# what `fleetd` puts on every failed command, and which command it was is `git`'s to change.
job failure
await sticky_error exists 30000
assert sticky_error ~= "^command failed: git"

shot sticky-error

# [A18]: `!` focuses the slot, which Fleet implements as opening the Jobs panel
# (`shell/root/actions.rs::focus_sticky_error`). It opens on row 0 rather than on the failed
# row, so the cursor is asserted where Fleet actually leaves it, not where [A18] reads.
key !
await overlay == Jobs 5000
assert key_contexts[0] == "Jobs" && focused == "jobs.row[0]"

# The panel lists the injected clone next to Fleet's own startup `Inspect worktrees`, and the
# slot keeps the error while the panel is open: a sticky error is dismissed by fixing the job,
# never by looking at it. `jobs` is not indexed here — its order is the daemon's, not submission
# order, and it has been observed both ways around.
assert lists.jobs.rows[1] exists && lists.jobs.selected exists && sticky_error exists
dump jobs

# The pointer does what `!` does: close the panel, then click the error itself.
key escape
await overlay absent 5000
assert sticky_error exists && targets["sticky_error.close"] exists
click sticky_error.retry
await overlay == Jobs 5000
assert key_contexts[0] == "Jobs" && sticky_error exists
key escape
await overlay absent 5000

# Its ✕ clears the slot; the failed jobs stay in the panel.
click sticky_error.close
await sticky_error absent 5000
assert targets["sticky_error.retry"] absent
key J
await overlay == Jobs 5000
assert lists.jobs.rows[1] exists
key escape
await overlay absent 5000

# `X` is the ✕'s key: retrying the clone fails the same way, which fills the slot again, and
# `X` clears it.
key J
await overlay == Jobs 5000
click jobs.filter[2]
await lists.jobs.filter == "failed"
click jobs.row[0].retry
await sticky_error exists 30000
key escape
await overlay absent 5000
key X
await sticky_error absent 5000
dump dismissed
quit
